The sky appears blue because of a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering. Sunlight is made up of all colors of the visible spectrum, and when it enters Earth's atmosphere, it collides with molecules of nitrogen and oxygen that are much smaller than the wavelength of visible light. Rayleigh scattering affects short wavelengths far more strongly than long ones—roughly inversely proportional to the fourth power of wavelength. Blue light, with its short wavelength, is scattered about ten times more efficiently than red light. This scattered blue light bounces in every direction across the sky, so no matter where you look overhead, some of it reaches your eyes. Violet light is scattered even more, but our eyes are more sensitive to blue, and some violet is absorbed in the upper atmosphere, so the sky reads as blue rather than purple.

